{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the snowball vs avalanche method?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The snowball method pays off the smallest balance first for quick wins, while the avalanche method targets the highest interest rate first to minimize total interest paid."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I know if debt consolidation is right for me?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Consolidation makes sense when you can get a lower interest rate than your current debts, you have a stable income for consistent payments, and your total debt is manageable within 3 to 5 years."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What interest rate should I expect for a consolidation loan?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Rates depend on credit score and lender. Borrowers with good credit (690+) typically qualify for 6% to 12%. Those with fair credit may see rates from 13% to 20%."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does consolidation reduce my total debt?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, consolidation reorganizes your debt, not reduces it. You still owe the same principal. The savings come from a lower interest rate, which reduces total interest paid over the loan term."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I include different types of debt in a consolidation loan?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, most consolidation loans can cover credit cards, medical bills, personal loans, and other unsecured debts. Secured debts like auto loans and mortgages are typically handled separately."}}]}
</script>
